.TH UCODE-LSP 1 "2026-08-09" "0.8.6" "ucode-lsp manual"
.SH NAME
ucode-lsp \- language server and static checker for ucode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ucode-lsp
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIpaths\fR...]
.br
.B ucode-lsp \-\-type-coverage
[\fIpaths\fR...]
.br
.B ucode-lsp \-\-stdio
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ucode-lsp
is a language server and static analysis tool for the ucode scripting
language used in OpenWrt.
.PP
When invoked without a transport flag, it scans \fB.uc\fR files and prints
diagnostics (errors and warnings) to stdout, similar to \fBtsc\fR.
When invoked with \fB\-\-stdio\fR, it runs as an LSP server for use with
editors like Neovim or VS Code. In server mode it also handles \fB.ut\fR
LuCI template files (the \fBucode-template\fR language) and template\-mode
\fB.uc\fR files (content starting with \fB{%\fR).
.SH MODES
.TP
.B (default)
Check mode. Scans \fB.uc\fR files in the given paths (or the current
directory) and prints diagnostics. Exits with code 1 if errors are found.
.TP
.B \-\-stdio
LSP server mode. Communicates over stdin/stdout using the Language Server
Protocol. Used by editors. \fB\-\-pipe\fR and \fB\-\-node\-ipc\fR select the
other LSP transports.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Show all diagnostics including info and hints. By default, only errors and
warnings are shown.
.TP
.B \-\-target-version \fIv\fR, \fB\-t \fIv\fR
Target OpenWrt/ucode release for version\-gated diagnostics. Language features
and module functions that a given release lacks are flagged when it is the
target (e.g. a module added in a later feed, or newer syntax). One of
\fB22.03\fR, \fB23.05\fR, \fB24.10\fR, \fB25.12\fR, or \fBmain\fR
(default: \fB25.12\fR). Choose \fBmain\fR to disable the version checks.
.TP
.B \-\-type-coverage
Instead of printing diagnostics, audit type coverage: report every variable
that has no hover or whose inferred type contains \fBunknown\fR. See
.B TYPE COVERAGE
below.
.TP
.B \-\-help-types
Print the type\-annotation guide (the JSDoc syntax described under
.B TYPE ANNOTATIONS )
and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a brief help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version number and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No errors found (warnings may still be present).
.TP
.B 1
One or more errors found.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage (unknown option, missing file, etc.).
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
Diagnostic lines are printed to stdout in the format:
.PP
.RS
\fIfile\fR(\fIline\fR,\fIcol\fR): \fIseverity\fR [\fIcode\fR]: \fImessage\fR
.RE
.PP
Summary and error messages are printed to stderr.
.SH TYPE COVERAGE
With \fB\-\-type-coverage\fR the tool does not print diagnostics. Instead it
probes every variable identifier through the same hover machinery an editor
uses and reports the ones the analyzer could not fully type \(em variables with
no hover, or whose inferred type still contains \fBunknown\fR. Each finding is
one line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fIfile\fR(\fIline\fR,\fIstartCol\fR\-\fIendCol\fR): unknown\-type: '\fIname\fR' shows as `\fItype\fR`
\fIfile\fR(\fIline\fR,\fIstartCol\fR\-\fIendCol\fR): no\-hover: '\fIname\fR'
.fi
.RE
.PP
A trailing summary reports the percentage of variable occurrences that are
fully typed. Unlike check mode, this always exits \fB0\fR \(em it is an audit,
not a pass/fail gate. Use it to find code that would benefit from
\fB@param\fR/\fB@typedef\fR annotations, or to surface gaps in inference.
.SH TYPE ANNOTATIONS
.B ucode-lsp
uses JSDoc comments to provide type information for static analysis.
Annotations must use the \fB/** ... */\fR comment style.
.SS @param \- Parameter Types
Annotate function parameters with their expected types:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/**
 * @param {string} name \- The user's name
 * @param {number} age
 */
function greet(name, age) { ... }
.fi
.RE
.PP
An alternative bare syntax is also supported:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/** @param name string \- The user's name */
.fi
.RE
.SS @returns \- Return Types
Document the return type of a function:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/** @returns {string} The greeting message */
function greet(name) { ... }
.fi
.RE
.PP
The annotation is checked against the function's actual return statements:
a declared type that does not cover what the body can return (for example a
missing \fBnull\fR arm, or a \fB{boolean}\fR on a value\-preserving
\fBguard && (...)\fR return) is reported as \fBUC7005\fR, usually with a
quick fix.
.SS @typedef / @property \- Custom Types
Define named object types with typed properties:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/**
 * @typedef {object} PkgInfo
 * @property {string} name \- Package name
 * @property {string} version \- Semantic version
 * @property {boolean?} installed \- Whether installed
 */

/** @param {PkgInfo} pkg */
function install(pkg) {
    pkg.name;       // autocomplete + type: string
    pkg.installed;  // type: boolean | null
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Typedefs are scoped to the file they are defined in.
.SS Supported Types
.TP
.B Primitives
\fBstring\fR, \fBnumber\fR, \fBinteger\fR (\fBint\fR), \fBdouble\fR (\fBfloat\fR),
\fBboolean\fR (\fBbool\fR), \fBarray\fR, \fBobject\fR, \fBfunction\fR, \fBnull\fR,
\fBregex\fR (\fBregexp\fR)
.TP
.B Generic arrays
\fBarray<T>\fR types the elements: \fB{array<string>}\fR makes \fBfor (let s
in arr)\fR type \fBs\fR as \fBstring\fR and \fBarr[i]\fR as
\fBstring | null\fR.
.TP
.B Module types
Builtin modules can be used directly as types:
\fBfs\fR, \fBuci\fR, \fBubus\fR, \fBuloop\fR, \fBmath\fR, \fBio\fR, \fBlog\fR,
\fBdebug\fR, \fBdigest\fR, \fBnl80211\fR, \fBresolv\fR, \fBrtnl\fR, \fBsocket\fR,
\fBstruct\fR, \fBzlib\fR, \fBbpf\fR, \fBhtml\fR, \fBlua\fR, \fBlucihttp\fR,
\fBpkgen\fR, \fBuclient\fR, \fBudebug\fR, \fBuline\fR.
The \fBmodule:\fR prefix is also accepted: \fB{module:fs}\fR.
.TP
.B Object types
Module object types provide method/property completion:
\fBfs.file\fR, \fBfs.dir\fR, \fBfs.proc\fR, \fBfs.stat\fR, \fBfs.statvfs\fR,
\fBio.handle\fR, \fBuci.cursor\fR, \fBuci.section\fR, \fBsocket\fR,
\fBubus.connection\fR, \fBubus.channel\fR, \fBubus.deferred\fR,
\fBubus.object\fR, \fBubus.request\fR, \fBubus.notify\fR, \fBubus.listener\fR,
\fBubus.subscriber\fR, \fBnl80211.listener\fR, \fBrtnl.listener\fR,
\fBuloop.timer\fR, \fBuloop.handle\fR, \fBuloop.process\fR, \fBuloop.task\fR,
\fBuloop.interval\fR, \fBuloop.signal\fR, \fBuloop.pipe\fR,
\fBstruct.instance\fR, \fBstruct.buffer\fR, \fBzlib.deflate\fR,
\fBzlib.inflate\fR, \fBbpf.module\fR, \fBbpf.map\fR, \fBbpf.program\fR,
\fBuline.state\fR, \fBuline.argp\fR, \fBmbedtls.pk\fR, \fBmbedtls.crt\fR,
\fBuhttpd\fR, \fBnetifd.proto\fR, \fBnetifd.daemon\fR, \fBhostapd.global\fR,
\fBhostapd.bss\fR, \fBhostapd.iface\fR, \fBwpas.global\fR, \fBwpas.iface\fR,
\fBluci.http\fR, \fBluci.dispatcher\fR, \fBexception\fR.
.TP
.B Union types
Combine types with \fB|\fR: \fB{string|number}\fR, \fB{fs.file|null}\fR.
.TP
.B Optional types
Append or prefix \fB?\fR to make a type nullable: \fB{string?}\fR and
\fB{?string}\fR are both equivalent to \fB{string|null}\fR.
.TP
.B Optional parameters
Bracket the name (\fB@param {string} [ctx]\fR) or use \fB{string=}\fR to mark
a parameter optional: call sites may omit it, and inside the function its type
gains \fB| null\fR.
.TP
.B import() expressions
Reference types from other modules:
.RS
.nf
{import('fs')}           \- builtin module type
{import('fs').file}      \- object type from module
{import('./config')}      \- default export from local file
{import('./config').name} \- named export or default export property
.fi
.RE
.SS Cross\-File Types with import()
You can reference types from other \fB.uc\fR files using import().
.PP
Named exports (\fBexport const\fR, \fBexport function\fR):
.PP
.RS
.nf
// lib/types.uc
export const config = {
    host: 'localhost',
    port: 8080,
    enabled: true,
};

// main.uc
/** @param {import('./lib/types').config} cfg */
function start(cfg) {
    cfg.host;     // autocomplete + type: string
    cfg.port;     // type: number
    cfg.enabled;  // type: boolean
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Default exports:
.PP
.RS
.nf
// lib/types.uc
export default {
    host: 'localhost',
    port: 8080,
};

// main.uc
/** @param {import('./lib/types')} config */
function start(config) {
    config.host;  // type: string
    config.port;  // type: number
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
You can also access a property of the default export:
.PP
.RS
.nf
// lib/types.uc
export default {
    db: { host: 'localhost', port: 5432 },
    app: { name: 'myapp', debug: false },
};

// main.uc
/** @param {import('./lib/types').db} conn */
function connect(conn) {
    conn.host;  // type: string
    conn.port;  // type: number
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
When a name matches both a named export and a default export property,
the named export takes priority.
.SS Strict Mode
When a file contains \fB'use strict'\fR, the analyzer emits info\-level
diagnostics (UC7003) for function parameters that lack \fB@param\fR
annotations. Use \fB\-\-verbose\fR in check mode to see these.
.SH DIAGNOSTIC CODES
Codes are grouped by family. The list below is representative, not exhaustive.
.SS Parser Errors
Syntax errors detected during parsing.
.SS Names and scope (UC1xxx)
.TP
.B UC1001
Undefined variable: a read of a name with no visible declaration.
.TP
.B UC1002
Undefined function: a call to a name that is not a callable in scope.
.TP
.B UC1005
Variable shadows a variable from an outer scope.
.TP
.B UC1006
Variable is declared but never used.
.TP
.B UC1009
Used before its declaration. ucode does not hoist function or variable
declarations, so a use above the declaration is a runtime error.
.SS Values and calls (UC2xxx)
.TP
.B UC2004
A builtin or module function is passed an argument of a provably wrong type.
.TP
.B UC2006, UC2007
printf/sprintf format string errors: wrong number of arguments, or an
argument whose type does not match its conversion specifier.
.TP
.B UC2009
Impossible comparison: a value whose type makes the comparison always true or
always false.
.TP
.B UC2010
A value that is not callable is called like a function.
.SS Syntax fidelity (UC6xxx)
Constructs the real ucode compiler rejects even though they scan as plausible
code \(em reserved words as identifiers, empty import/export lists, bare
numeric object keys, and similar. Each verdict is verified against the ucode
interpreter.
.SS Imports and modules (UC3xxx)
.TP
.B UC3002
Cannot find the imported module.
.TP
.B UC3006
Call to a module function that is not imported, or not a member of the module.
.TP
.B UC3007
An \fBimport\fR statement that is not at the top level of the file.
.TP
.B UC3008
\fBrequire()\fR with a relative path (\fB"./x"\fR). ucode's \fBrequire\fR
resolves only search\-path names; a relative path throws at runtime.
.SS Control flow (UC4xxx)
.TP
.B UC4001
Unreachable code after return, break, continue, or die()/exit().
.TP
.B UC4005
A loop mutates the collection it iterates. ucode iterates arrays by a live
index, so shrinking (pop/shift/splice) silently skips elements and growing
(push/unshift) may not terminate. An unconditional growth in a loop with no
exit is a provable infinite loop and is reported as an error.
.SS Null safety (UC5xxx)
.TP
.B UC5004
Access to a property that is not a known member of the object.
.TP
.B UC5005
Property access or indexing on a value that is provably null here.
.TP
.B UC5006
Property access or indexing on a value that may be null here.
.SS JSDoc annotations (UC7xxx)
.TP
.B UC7001
Unknown type in an annotation \(em with a did\-you\-mean suggestion and
rewrite fix when a close match exists (\fB?Socket\fR \(-> \fB?socket\fR).
.TP
.B UC7002
A @param name does not match any function parameter.
.TP
.B UC7003
Function parameters missing @param annotations (strict mode only). The quick
fix can generate a full \fB@typedef\fR from how the parameter's members are
actually used.
.TP
.B UC7005
A declared @returns type that does not cover what the function's body can
actually return.
.SS Global\-scope soundness and risky calls (UC8xxx)
.TP
.B UC8001
Unguarded call to a builtin that throws on bad input (json(), loadfile(),
loadstring(), require(), render()) outside a try/catch.
.TP
.B UC8002
Read of a global before it is assigned in this file.
.TP
.B UC8004, UC8005
A global whose existence at a later read cannot be statically determined
(every assignment sits on a path that may not run), and reads of such a global.
.SS Argument checks
.TP
.B incompatible\-function\-argument
Argument type does not match what the function expects.
.TP
.B nullable\-argument
Argument may be null where a non\-null value is required.
.SH EDITOR SETUP
.SS Neovim (0.11+)
Add to \fB~/.config/nvim/init.lua\fR:
.PP
.RS
.nf
vim.filetype.add({ extension = { uc = 'ucode' } })

vim.lsp.config('ucode', {
  cmd = { 'ucode-lsp', '\-\-stdio' },
  filetypes = { 'ucode' },
  root_markers = { '.git' },
})
vim.lsp.enable('ucode')
.fi
.RE
.SS VS Code
Install the \fBucode Language Support\fR extension from the VS Code
marketplace.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check all .uc files in the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B ucode-lsp
.RE
.PP
Check a specific directory:
.PP
.RS
.B ucode-lsp src/
.RE
.PP
Check a single file with verbose output:
.PP
.RS
.B ucode-lsp \-\-verbose router.uc
.RE
.PP
Check against a specific OpenWrt release's ucode:
.PP
.RS
.B ucode-lsp \-\-target-version 23.05 router.uc
.RE
.PP
Audit how much of the code is fully typed:
.PP
.RS
.B ucode-lsp \-\-type-coverage src/
.RE
.PP
Pipe diagnostics to count errors:
.PP
.RS
.B ucode-lsp src/ | grep \-c error
.RE
